I like YouTube, even if most of its users are depressingly inane.
then someone else will come along in however much time and start using
them again.

And it's going to be exactly what he deserves. I can't honestly think of
a single thing on the internet that can be physically stolen and become
irreplacable. I've got to jump through their hoops.
Typically there are a hundred or so Ogre NPCs gathered around the base
of the arena tower dancing about. It's basic copyright. But, to take the
example of Tur's product, these aren't things that you could for
instance find in a blockbuster. Is this actually happening? This
argument will hold water the day someone is convicted of theft when
they've only been infringing copyrights. Taking a photo of a painting is
somewhat different to taking the physical painting. By any reasonable
stretch, people ought to be in the streets demanding Bush's impeachment,
along with Cheney.

No, it must be a technical violation, something like the warrantless
surveillance.

That's what the RIAA has done for me. Then something important occurred
to me. It needs to be taught a lesson. Amounts to the same thing,
though: money out of the copyright holder's pocket.
It's not enough to email. because he is seeking more institutionally to
cripple checks and balances and the authority of Congress and the
judiciary to superintend his assertions of power.
You may not be a big thief, but I think it's important that we call
things by their names. Do you work for a living? Just as the owners of
Pets. I am not so sure anymore. I think they're a little bit more
worrisome than Clinton's.
Maybe I should get paid for a page of text, and paragraphs go free?
And I'd never seen the one amateur looking video in there "Dance First".
Amounts to the same thing, though: money out of the copyright holder's
pocket. Whether or not it is OK is a different question, but I don't
think the distinction in conduct is that difficult to comprehend.
If anything is fucked up here, it's our copyright laws. They are
complying with the terms of the DMCA. But if it's rampantly copied and
freely available, that value drops to near zero, because no one is
interested in purchasing it from you.

